<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Wilberforce</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1833">1833</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Wilberforce was an English politician and evangelical Christian who led the parliamentary campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Member of Parliament for Hull and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, he introduced abolition bills from 1789 until the Slave Trade Act was passed in 1807. He then campaigned for the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> itself, and the Slavery Abolition Act passed in 1833, days after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce.jpg" alt="William Wilberforce" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Hickel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_wilberforce.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
